# Project Architecture – NoursProject

> The environment is intentionally permissive at the network level to support full attack and detection visibility.

## Overview
This project represents a cloud-based security monitoring and detection lab deployed on Vultr. 
It includes centralized logging with ELK, adversary simulation using Mythic C2, and Windows/Linux target systems.
The environment is designed for controlled testing, detection validation, and alert-to-ticket workflows.

---

## Cloud Environment

- **Cloud Provider:** Vultr
- **Deployment Model:** Multi-VM cloud lab

### Deployed Instances

| VM Name                  | OS       | Role |
|--------------------------|----------|------|
| NoursProject-ELK         | Ubuntu   | Log aggregation, SIEM, alerting |
| NoursProject-FLEET       | Ubuntu   | Fleet / agent management |
| NoursProject-MYTHIC      | Ubuntu   | C2 server for adversary simulation |
| NoursProject-Linux       | Ubuntu   | Linux target system |
| NoursProject-WIN         | Windows  | Windows target system |
| NoursProject-osTicket    | Windows  | Ticketing and incident tracking |

---

## Networking & Access Model

- All servers are deployed in Vultr within isolated cloud environments.
- Public IPs are assigned for lab access and testing purposes.
- **Broad port ranges (TCP 1–65335)** are allowed at the cloud firewall level **only for lab simulation and testing**.

### Rationale
Wide port access was intentionally configured to:
- Avoid port-related interference during attack simulation
- Allow dynamic C2 communication
- Enable full visibility of network activity for detection engineering
- Focus on monitoring and alerting rather than perimeter blocking

---

## Security Controls

Although wide port ranges are allowed at the cloud firewall level:

- Access is restricted using:
  - Strong authentication
  - SSH keys (Linux)
  - Controlled credentials (Windows)
- Servers are dedicated to lab use only
- No production or sensitive data is hosted
- Logging and monitoring are enabled across all systems

This design reflects a **detection-first security model**, where visibility and response validation are prioritized over strict network filtering.

---

## Traffic Flow Summary

- Attack traffic originates from controlled attacker systems
- Logs are forwarded to the ELK stack
- Detection rules trigger alerts in Kibana
- Alerts are automatically forwarded to osTicket
- Tickets are reviewed and investigated by the analyst

---

## Notes

This architecture is intentionally permissive to support:
- SOC training
- Detection engineering
- Cloud security experimentation

In a production environment, strict port restrictions and network segmentation would be applied.
